
   RockLisp (Version 2) by Rocket Software  Copyright 1991
   This shareware package contains:


        1.  A number of Lisp files for use with AutoDesk's AutoCAD
            drafting package.
        2.  The file Index.doc which is an alphabetical listing of
            the Lisp files with a brief explanation of each (and the
            occasional editorial comment).
        3.  The file Sample.pgp which is an explanation of AutoCAD's
            Acad.pgp file for those who have yet to fathom its fairly
            simple intricacies.
        4.  The file Custom.doc which contains a few thoughts (a brief
            tirade, actually) on AutoCAD customization.
        5.  The file Briefndx.doc which contains a one-line description
            of each lisp routine so that you can find the one you want
            if the name escapes you.

  A few notes:
  
        1.  These routines were written for a variety of purposes.  They
            are not all intended to be universally useful, so don't be
            alarmed if there seem to be a lot which are of no use to you.
            There are also a certain number of routines which seem to be
            merely variations on a theme, which they are: not everyone
            wants to start modifying programs, and I would rather write
            a close variation than clutter up a routine with endless inputs
            in an attempt to anticipate every conceivable eventuality.

        2.  If you are of a serious turn of mind please forgive those
            files which are purely frivolous.  Similarly persons in
            positions of authority could be benificent enough to
            ignore those which seem aimed solely at undermining their
            control of the masses.

        3.  Common needs breed common solutions.  I know of at least
            one item contained herein which has been duplicated elsewhere,
            there may be others.

        4.  These programs are ready to use as is, but as I have stated in
            a number of places, you are welcome to cut and chop them to
            suit your needs.  If you lack either the inclination or the
            time I could probably be persuaded by an interesting idea
            or a small amount of cash.


  Miscellaneous ramblings:

  The procedure, before I forget, for loading Lisp files is to type at the
Acad "Command:" prompt: (load "filename"). If you are loading for outside
the current directory you will have to add a path, as in (load "A:\filename").
You don't have to add the ".lsp" extension, but it doesn't hurt.
If you like a Lisp routine well enough to want to load it with each drawing,
copy it into the Acad.lsp file, it will then load automatically each time
you enter the drawing editor. You should try to keep from dumping everything
even vaguely interesting in, though: the larger the file the longer it takes
to load, and even on a fast computer a large Acad.lsp can take an irritating
amount of time.

  There are other procedures to load files only if they are actually needed,
such as putting: (IF (NOT C:UC) (LOAD "C:/ACAD/UC.LSP")) in the menu
structure before the appropriate command - in this case "UC".  These are
detailed in a variety of books on AutoCAD modification, and I will not
duplicate them here.  (I would like to recommend a book but the majority of
them seem dedicated not to teaching the user the methods he requires but to
convincing him to construct some elaborate structure they have devised for
purposes he most likely does not share.)

  Also it is worth reading the Lisp manual on the (S::STARTUP) command:
basically you place (S::STARTUP () various stuff) inside ACAD.LSP in the
same format as (DEFUN C:ROUTINENAME () various stuff), and as soon as the
drawing editor is ready to go it runs the routine automatically; thus you
can use (S::STARTUP () (C:QUONSET)) and AutoCAD will automatically run
Quonset when you load a drawing, (assuming that Quonset is already loaded).
See also the notes on L.lsp in Index.doc which detail another method of lisp
loading.

  Although I have tested each of these routines on several computers and
would obviously not release any which was not to the best of my knowledge
both complete and harmless, it is a good idea to save any drawing before
trying a previously untested procedure of any type, i.e. don't shell out
of Autocad after three hours of otherwise unrecorded inspiration and then
run a virus removal program.

  Speaking of viruses, the computer on which this was written is tested
frequently and has so far always been free of them. For those who think that
viruses are not of much concern or interest, I agreed wholeheartedly until
quite recently.  (Even if the thought of having to format your hard disk and
then dig up new copies of all that software you never use doesn't dismay
you, possibly the thought of being lynched by your associates who think you
infected them might give you pause.)

